    <content>
      <![CDATA[<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="no"?>
<!DOCTYPE en-note SYSTEM "http://xml.evernote.com/pub/enml2.dtd">
<en-note><blockquote>my note: SF is not hot but Oct can surprise. if race morning &gt; 65F, start at 6:35 not 6:32.</blockquote><hr/><h3>Heat and humidity: adjusting pace for fall races</h3><div>Trail &amp; Track Weekly, August 2026</div><div><br/></div><div>Fall race season starts in weather that can still be warm. A common rule of thumb: above roughly 60°F, add about 1 to 2 percent to goal pace for every 5°F, more if the humidity is high or the dew point is above 60.</div><div><br/></div><div>Heat acclimation takes about 10 to 14 days of easy running in warm conditions. Most of the benefit (a larger plasma volume, earlier sweating, lower heart rate at a given pace) arrives in the first week. If your race may be warm but your training is cool, a few easy sessions in extra layers can help, but don't do workouts that way.</div><div><br/></div><div>On race day: pour water on your head and neck early, not when you already feel hot; take fluids at every station on warm days; and start conservatively. In the heat, the runners who go out slow pass a lot of people in the last 5K.</div></en-note>]]>
    </content>
  </note>
</en-export>
